arguably more great goalkeepers play in  the bundesliga than in any other league  germany is known for producing huge  talents between the sticks  but there is something perverse about  the position  the goalkeeper was a specific type of  exile a scapegoat always handily there  to take the blame for the rest of the  team  it used to be that the goalkeeper was  the clumsiest kid on the playground the  one picked last when teams were divided  up  sports journalist jonathan wilson has  written a book about the history of the  position  the outsider offers an inside look at  the loneliest job in sport  keepers he says weren't always outcasts  the goalkeeper initially he was part of  the team he wore the same colored shirt  and although he could handle the ball he  was essentially just another player and  you look at the the very first rules in  1863 and there's no no reference to a  goalkeeper any player could handle the  ball could catch the ball and was  allowed a free kick now that changes  over the end of the 19th century and  finally in 1908 the goalkeeper was given  a different coloured shirt it takes  almost 50 years before he's really  pulled apart from the rest of the team  with the change of jersey goalkeepers  gained an identity and some of them  became stars gordon banks ricardo zamora  leviathan  tony turek  and bear troutman  i think trenton's story i mean it's one  of the most extraordinary stories not  just in football but one of the most  extraordinary stories of the 20th  century his experiences  uh you know in the war of becoming a  prisoner of war in england and then  adapting to english life and he was  really sort of the first popular german  after the second world war in england  and i think it says much about his  charisma and his personality and how  good he was as a goalkeeper not just not  only was he a great keeper but he was in  involved in one of the  most iconic moments  of the fa cup that he he broke his neck  near the end of a cup final and and  played on so he be you you sort of saw  the heroism  the possibility of becoming a hero or a  scapegoat distinguishes the keeper from  the rest of the team a few years back  kickoff talked to former u.s number one  casey keller about what qualities  goalkeepers need his answer was frank  stupidity of being a goalkeeper what  stupidity is it stupidity it's  it's not a fun  it's not a fun job sometimes you're part  of the team and sometimes you're not  part of the team  we train separately  most of the time  if a goalkeeper makes a mistake it tends  to be catastrophic it tends to cost a  goal if you're a full-back you can make  a mistake and maybe the winger messes up  the cross maybe somebody covers for you  you'll send it forward you miss a chance  you get another chance people forget  that if you're a goalkeeper you drop a  cross and somebody puts it in or you let  a shot slide under your body obviously  remember because it results in a goal  even one of the most formidable keepers  of all time the titan ollie khan new  moments of absolute despair  one mistake is all it takes for a  goalkeeper no matter how good to make  negative headlines  you have to have a robust psychology you  have to clean up everyone else's  mistakes and not get unduly distracted  by your own ones  sometimes goals simply happen it's part  of the game  but then everyone turns his back on you  because they have to go back to the  middle of the pitch  situations like that perhaps mess with  your subconscious so the goalkeeper kind  of has to be like a terminator  you could compare it with a surgeon  performing a difficult operation who's  completely drained after three or four  hours even though he hasn't exerted  himself physically the mental work is  very exhausting  and then there are the moments when the  referee blows his whistle his stony  demeanor brooks no objections as he  points to the spot  what follows is the ultimate showdown in  football  i think the penalty it breaks football  down into its absolute purest form  there's a man trying to score a goal  there's a ball there's a man trying to  stop him scoring a goal  and so  it's absolutely pure it's the essence of  football  goalkeepers in the relish penalties  because nobody expects them to save them  and the usual polarities are reversed  concede a penalty and it's one of those  things save one and you become a hero  an inversion of a goalkeeper's usual  role as a scapegoat  modern goalkeepers are also expected to  have good skills with their feet  and then i think that that takes an  another leap uh with the introduction  the back pass law in in 92 that from  then  goalkeepers have to be good with both  feet they they can't be people who  effectively can't kick the ball they've  got to be able to deal with the ball  being passed back to them and gradually  i think we've recognized that the  benefits goalkeepers can have they  dispute the ball well whether that be  with a long throw or a long kick i think  um certainly the last 10 years or so  there's probably no country has produced  so many good goalkeepers as germany and  manuel neuer  know he recently voted the best  goalkeeper in the world and i think very  few people would argue with that annoyer  i think is is a great example of this  this idea of the sweeper keeper somebody  who if the defense pushed out the back  four  pushed out somebody who could sweep up  behind to cut out the the risk of three  balls in that space um i remember  occasionally last euros the game against  italy as as germany were pushing up  desperately seeking the equalizer and  there's neuer in the center circle  heading the ball he's so far out of his  goal but you know totally confident  totally capable of doing that and  allowing the defense to push right up  but even neuer makes the occasional  mistake and when he does everyone is  quick to criticize  the life of a goalkeeper is uncertain  good goalkeepers have reputations  damaged by one unfortunate mistake  others are thrust into the limelight by  one or two saves there's a randomness  about it that is unsettling  bad things happen to good people and we  don't necessarily get what we deserve  goalkeepers may not get the recognition  they deserve but with your help we'd  like to recognize one member of the  german footballing family  you
 

